# What Does `new self()` Mean in PHP?

You often encounter this pattern in Singleton implementations or Factory methods:

```php
public static function getInstance()
{
    if (!isset(self::$_instance)) {
        self::$_instance = new self();
    }
    return self::$_instance;
}
```

At first glance, it looks like a shorthand for `new ClassName()`. But in PHP, `self` is not just a variable you can swap with any class name. It refers to the class where the code *currently executes*, which changes depending on how the method is called and whether inheritance is involved.

When you write `new self()` inside a class, PHP resolves `self` at runtime relative to the calling context. If the method is defined in `A` and called directly on `A`, then `self` refers to `A`. But if `A` extends `B` and an instance of `B` calls that same method, `self` still points to `A` because the code physically lives inside `A`. This can lead to surprising behavior when designing inheritance hierarchies.

Consider this simple hierarchy:

```php
class Base
{
    protected static ?Base $instance = null;

    public static function getInstance(): Base
    {
        if (!self::$instance) {
            self::$instance = new self(); // self always means the class where this code lives
        }
        return self::$instance;
    }
}

class Child extends Base
{
    protected static ?Child $childInstance = null;

    public static function getBaseInstance(): Base
    {
        if (!self::$instance) { // This is still Base::$instance, because the code lives in Base
            self::$instance = new self(); // Creates a new Base instance
        }
        return self::$instance;
    }

    public static function getChildInstance(): Child
    {
        if (!self::$childInstance) {
            self::$childInstance = new self(); // This creates a new Child instance
        }
        return self::$childInstance;
    }
}
```

Notice how `Base::getInstance()` always returns a `Base` object, even when called from `Child`. That's because the method lives in `Base`, so `self` resolves to `Base` at runtime. If you want polymorphic behaviorâwhere `Child::getBaseInstance()` returns a `Child` instead of `Base`âyou would need to change the logic entirely, perhaps by using `static` or by restructuring how the shared state is stored.

Using `static` changes the resolution to late static binding:

```php
public static function getInstance()
{
    if (!isset(static::$instance)) {
        static::$instance = new static(); // now respects inheritance
    }
    return static::$instance;
}
```

With `static`, calling `Child::getInstance()` will instantiate a `Child` object instead of `Base`. This is the standard approach for type-safe singletons in hierarchies. The trade-off is that you must ensure `static::$instance` exists in each subclass if you want them to share separate state, or use a shared property carefully across the hierarchy.

One common pitfall is mixing `self` and `static` properties. If you define `$instance` as `protected static ?Base $instance = null;` in `Base`, then both `Base` and `Child` will write to the same property because they share the same name and scope unless you override it with a different property (as shown above). That can cause subtle bugs when you expect subclass-specific state.

Another consideration is serialization and shared state across processes. If your singleton stores global data, make sure that `self` vs `static` behavior doesn't break assumptions about which class owns the state. In microservices or multi-instance setups, this distinction becomes even more important.

If you're building a framework or a reusable component where inheritance is expected, prefer `static` for late binding and document the contract clearly: does the method return the exact type of the caller, or always the base type? That decision determines whether `self` or `static` is appropriate.

For Laravel developers, this distinction matters less in most cases because Laravel's service container handles object instantiation and resolution automatically. However, when writing custom singletons, factories, or plugin-like classes that may be extended, understanding how `self` resolves helps you avoid breaking changes in subclasses. You can read more about dependency injection patterns in Laravel at https://laravelcompany.com to see how modern frameworks abstract away these manual singleton concerns.

In summary, `new self()` always creates an instance of the class where the method is defined, regardless of which subclass calls it. Use `static` when you need the constructor to match the actual called class. Choose your resolution strategy based on whether you want a fixed base type or polymorphic behavior across your inheritance tree.
